1
00:06:08,571 --> 00:06:14,571
Life out of balance

2
00:12:17,142 --> 00:12:23,142
Koyaanisqatsi is a documentary, music film directed by Godfrey Reggio.

3
00:18:25,714 --> 00:18:31,714
Takes us to locations all around the US and shows us the heavy toll
that modern technology is having on humans and the earth.

4
00:24:34,285 --> 00:24:40,285
The visual tone poem contains neither dialogue nor a vocalized narration: its tone is set
by the juxtaposition of images and the exceptional music by Philip Glass.

5
00:30:42,857 --> 00:30:48,857
Koyaanisqatsi is a 1982 American non-narrative documentary film directed and produced by
Godfrey Reggio, featuring music by Philip Glass and cinematography by Ron Fricke.

6
00:36:51,428 --> 00:36:57,428
Described as an "essay in images and sound on the state of American civilization", the film comprises a montage of stock
footage, slow motion, and time-lapse visuals of natural and urban environments across the United States.

7
00:43:00,000 --> 00:43:06,000
Following its premieres at the Telluride and New York Film Festivals in
1982, it began a limited theatrical release the next year.

8
00:49:08,571 --> 00:49:14,571
Produced on a budget of $2.5 million, the film grossed $3.2 million at the box
office, and was one of the highest-grossing documentaries of the 1980s.

9
00:55:17,142 --> 00:55:23,142
The title comes from the Hopi word koyaanisqatsi, meaning "life out of balance".

10
01:01:25,714 --> 01:01:31,714
It is the first film in the Qatsi trilogy, which was followed
by Powaqqatsi (1988) and Naqoyqatsi (2002).

11
01:07:34,285 --> 01:07:40,285
The trilogy depicts different aspects of the relationship
between humans, nature and technology.

12
01:13:42,857 --> 01:13:48,857
Koyaanisqatsi is the best known of the trilogy and is considered a cult film.

13
01:19:51,428 --> 01:19:57,428
The film explores themes of human vs nature, technology,
civilization, social commentary, rural area.
